```jsx
import { useEffect, useId, useRef } from "react";
import { createPortal } from "react-dom";

const FOCUSABLE = [
  "a[href]",
  "button:not([disabled])",
  "input:not([disabled])",
  "select:not([disabled])",
  "textarea:not([disabled])",
  '[contenteditable="true"]',
  '[tabindex]:not([tabindex="-1"])',
].join(",");

export function Modal({ open, onClose, title, description, children }) {
  const titleId = useId();
  const descId = useId();
  const dialogRef = useRef(null);
  const triggerRef = useRef(null);
  const onCloseRef = useRef(onClose);

  useEffect(() => {
    onCloseRef.current = onClose;
  });

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!open) return;

    const dialog = dialogRef.current;
    const root = document.getElementById("root");
    triggerRef.current = document.activeElement;

    // 1. Make the rest of the page inert (removes it from tab order AND the a11y tree)
    root?.setAttribute("inert", "");
    root?.setAttribute("aria-hidden", "true"); // belt-and-braces for older AT

    // 2. Move focus into the dialog
    const focusables = () =>
      [...dialog.querySelectorAll(FOCUSABLE)].filter(
        (el) => el.getClientRects().length > 0
      );

    (focusables()[0] ?? dialog).focus();

    const onKeyDown = (e) => {
      if (e.key === "Escape") {
        e.preventDefault();
        e.stopPropagation();
        onCloseRef.current();
        return;
      }
      if (e.key !== "Tab") return;

      const items = focusables();
      if (items.length === 0) {
        e.preventDefault();
        dialog.focus();
        return;
      }
      const first = items[0];
      const last = items[items.length - 1];

      if (e.shiftKey && (document.activeElement === first || document.activeElement === dialog)) {
        e.preventDefault();
        last.focus();
      } else if (!e.shiftKey && document.activeElement === last) {
        e.preventDefault();
        first.focus();
      }
    };

    // 3. Recover focus if it escapes by mouse, AT, or a rogue autofocus
    const onFocusIn = (e) => {
      if (!dialog.contains(e.target)) dialog.focus();
    };

    document.addEventListener("keydown", onKeyDown, true);
    document.addEventListener("focusin", onFocusIn, true);

    return () => {
      document.removeEventListener("keydown", onKeyDown, true);
      document.removeEventListener("focusin", onFocusIn, true);
      root?.removeAttribute("inert");
      root?.removeAttribute("aria-hidden");
      // 4. Restore focus to whatever opened the dialog
      if (triggerRef.current?.isConnected) triggerRef.current.focus();
    };
  }, [open]);

  if (!open) return null;

  return createPortal(
    <div
      className="modal-backdrop"
      onMouseDown={(e) => {
        if (e.target === e.currentTarget) onCloseRef.current();
      }}
    >
      <div
        ref={dialogRef}
        role="dialog"
        aria-modal="true"
        aria-labelledby={titleId}
        aria-describedby={description ? descId : undefined}
        tabIndex={-1}
        className="modal"
      >
        <h2 id={titleId}>{title}</h2>
        {description && <p id={descId}>{description}</p>}
        <button type="button" onClick={onCloseRef.current} aria-label="Close dialog">
          <span aria-hidden="true">×</span>
        </button>
        {children}
      </div>
    </div>,
    document.body
  );
}
```

## Why these choices

**Labelled title.** `aria-labelledby` points at the real `<h2>`, so the accessible name is the visible text and stays in sync — no duplicated `aria-label` to drift out of date. `aria-describedby` is wired only when a description exists; an empty referenced node produces a confusing "blank" announcement in some screen readers.

**`role="dialog"` + `aria-modal="true"`.** `aria-modal` tells assistive tech to treat everything outside the dialog as unavailable. It is only *honest* if the outside content is actually unreachable — hence the `inert` below. Declaring `aria-modal` without hiding the background is a common and harmful bug: sighted keyboard users are trapped while screen reader users can still wander out.

**`inert` on the app root, not `aria-hidden` alone.** `inert` removes the subtree from the tab order, pointer events, and the accessibility tree in one attribute. `aria-hidden` only hides it from AT, leaving it keyboard-focusable — the exact recipe for a keyboard trap. I keep `aria-hidden` as a fallback for older engines but `inert` is doing the real work.

**Focus trap.** The `keydown` handler wraps Tab at the two boundaries rather than preventing Tab everywhere. This is the difference between a *containment* trap and a *keyboard trap*: every control inside remains reachable in both directions. Zero focusable children is handled by focusing the dialog itself (`tabIndex={-1}`) instead of silently swallowing Tab.

**No keyboard trap.** Escape always closes from anywhere inside the dialog, so there is always a documented exit. The `focusin` guard pulls focus back if it lands outside (mouse click on the backdrop, VoiceOver cursor movement, a stray `autofocus`), but it never blocks the user from leaving *via* Escape or the close button.

**Focus restoration.** The trigger is captured at open time, not at close time (`document.activeElement` at cleanup could already be `body`). The `isConnected` check prevents focusing a node that unmounted while the dialog was open.

**Close button name.** The `×` glyph is `aria-hidden` and the button carries `aria-label="Close dialog"`. A bare "×" is announced as "times" or not at all, and "Close" alone is ambiguous when several dialogs exist.

**Initial focus.** First focusable element is a reasonable default; for long or destructive dialogs, focus the dialog container or the least destructive action instead, and add `autoFocus` to a specific element when the content has a natural starting point.

**Portal to `document.body`.** The dialog must be a sibling of `#root` for `inert` on `#root` to hide the page without hiding the dialog. Portaling inside `#root` would make the modal itself inert.

**Escape handled in the capture phase** so it wins over nested handlers, and `stopPropagation` prevents a parent dialog or router from also reacting. For stacked modals, only the topmost instance should respond — track a stack rather than letting every open modal close on one Escape.

**`onMouseDown` on the backdrop, not `onClick`.** A click that starts inside the dialog and ends on the backdrop (text selection drag) would otherwise close the dialog unintentionally.

**Callback identity.** `onClose` is read through a ref so an inline arrow function from the parent doesn't tear down and rebuild the effect on every render, which would re-run focus capture and restore mid-interaction.

**Consider native `<dialog>`.** `showModal()` gives you the focus trap, Escape handling, backdrop, and focus restoration for free, with far less code to get wrong. The custom version above is worth it only when you need full styling control or must support environments where `<dialog>` is polyfilled inconsistently — and if you do use native `<dialog>`, keep the `aria-labelledby` and explicit close button name, since the element does not supply them.

